    "doi": "https://doi.org/10.1038/srep08280",
    "abstract": null,
    "field": "Global nitrogen cycling",
    "problem": "Understanding global nitrogen cycling by linking soil nitrogen isotopes directly to climate is challenged because previous models have overlooked the confounding, co-varying effects of soil carbon and clay concentrations, potentially leading to flawed interpretations of nitrogen loss pathways.",
    "method": "A global dataset of over 6000 soil samples was compiled to analyze the relationships between soil nitrogen isotopes, climate, and soil properties using non-linear regression and structural equation models to test for direct versus indirect climatic controls.",
    "task": "The task is to quantify global patterns of soil nitrogen isotopes by synthesizing data from over 6000 samples to understand their relationships with climate gradients, soil carbon, and clay concentrations.",
    "model": [
        "WorldClim"
    ],
